#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbcc16b7-dd31-55ce-ba1e-02b87263453c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Dimension of a Cylinder Given Its Volume"}] :question/id #uuid "e61b5943-210d-5ef3-9c71-5780762b0163" :question/math-academy-id "e-7182" :question/problem "Calculate the radius $r$ of the cylinder below given that its volume is $200\\pi \\,\\textrm{mm}^3$ and its height is $2\\,\\textrm{mm}$.\n\n![](images/d0/d0450b9c0f3ab8b6f5f0cf09c4ca21dac75d0077fe8b44bf740eb4dc198decfe.png)" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV = 200\\pi \\,\\textrm{mm}^3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh = 2\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula and solving for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= πr^{2}h \\\\\n200π &= πr^{2}(2) \\\\\n2πr^{2} &= 200π \\\\\nr^{2} &= 100 \\\\\nr &= 10mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdf7b0cd-95b8-5cc6-94c4-768ec04c973a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Cylinder"}] :question/id #uuid "edf5a890-3d4f-5917-b6a5-b1e3ff055fa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7185" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of a cylinder given that the circumference of the base is $10\\pi \\,\\textrm{m}$ and the ratio of its height to its radius is $\\dfrac{4}{5}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the radius $r$ using the formula $C=2\\pi r$, where $C$ is the circumference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πr \\\\\n10π &= 2πr \\\\\nr &= 5m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the height $h$, we use the fact that the ratio of the height to the radius is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{h}{r} &= \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n\\frac{h}{5} &= \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\nh &= 4m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2h\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr = 5\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh = 4\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV = \\pi(5)^2(4) = 100\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "234b3cbc-1b13-50fc-9bec-e2149cfb2ba7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for a Dimension of a Cylinder Given a Proportion"}] :question/id #uuid "af065541-e7e0-5f34-a566-40de5b546f0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7217" :question/problem "Find an expression for the height $h$ of a cylinder in terms of its volume $V$ given that the ratio of the radius of the base to the height of the cylinder is $4$." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the ratio of the radius $r$ of the base to the height $h$ is $4$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{r}{h} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to express $h$ in terms of $V$. Therefore, we need to isolate $r$ in the above equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{r}{h} &= 4 \\\\\nr &= 4h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting $r = 4h$ into the formula and solving for $h$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= πr^{2}h \\\\\nV &= π(4h)^{2}h \\\\\nV &= 16πh^{3} \\\\\nh^{3} &= \\frac{V}{16π} \\\\\nh &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{V}{16π}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdf7b0cd-95b8-5cc6-94c4-768ec04c973a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccca1f8e-ec54-5ed5-938e-d55325e15a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40πin^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094286} {:answer/id #uuid "fd897bea-1d73-58a3-b5a8-f1ac5476c109" :answer/ty